@* RadioGroup's Overview, written by hand after Button's, Card's and MultiSelect's: an enum's
   options stacked or in a row, options from a list, a required choice that starts empty, and
   states. Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>For two to five short options, a <Anchor Href="docs/components/segmented-control">SegmentedControl</Anchor> takes less room; for a long list, use a <Anchor Href="docs/components/select">Select</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Stacked or in a row</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Orientation.Horizontal</Code> to put a few short options in a row, wrapping when there isn't room; they stack by default. An enum's values are the options, named by their <Code>[Display(Name)]</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="radio-group-horizontal" />

<Heading Level="2">Options from a list</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Items</Code> for options that aren't an enum, and <Code>ItemText</Code> for how each one reads.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="radio-group-shipping" />
<TextBlock>Each radio posts its value as text and Blazor turns it back into the property's type, so use values that do that, such as strings, numbers or enums. A post can carry a value that isn't among the options, and it reaches the model, so check it on the server.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Required, with nothing chosen</Heading>
<TextBlock>Bind a nullable property with <Code>[Required]</Code> to start with nothing chosen and need a choice.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="radio-group-required-choice" />
<TextBlock>A property that isn't nullable always has a value, so <Code>[Required]</Code> never fails: the option for its starting value (an enum's <Code>0</Code>, unless the model sets another) is chosen from the start. The example also turns away a posted number that isn't one of the enum's values.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes, read-only and disabled</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Size</Code> to line up with buttons and other fields, <Code>ReadOnly</Code> for a choice that posts but can't change, and <Code>Disabled</Code> for one that doesn't post.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="radio-group-states" />
<TextBlock>HTML has no read-only radio, so a hidden field posts a read-only group's value; a crafted post can change it like any other, so check it on the server if it matters. A disabled field posts nothing, so after a static post its property holds its default (an enum's <Code>0</Code>, or null): keep its value on the server rather than saving it from the post.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        @* An enum: its values are the options, named by their [Display(Name)]. *@
        <RadioGroup @bind-Value="Model!.Digest" />

        @* Other values, with ItemText for each one's text. *@
        <RadioGroup @bind-Value="Model!.Rate" Items="Rates.Keys" ItemText="RateText"
                    Orientation="Orientation.Horizontal" />
        """;
}
